<body>
    <h1>Computer Vision Concepts</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>In this article, we=E2=80=99re going to discuss the concept of C=
omputer Vision. To make things easier, we=E2=80=99ve broken it down into a =
few parts that=E2=80=99ll help explain:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Why Computer Vision is important</strong></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>How an image is analyzed using Computer Vision</strong></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>What Computer Vision is used for</strong></p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionConcepts-WhyisComputerVisionimportant?">Why is Comp=
uter Vision important?</h2>
<p>Computer Vision is a field within artificial intelligence that uses <em>=
machine learning</em> and neural networks to help computers process and ext=
ract information from digital images. Basically, it=E2=80=99s about modelin=
g and reproducing human visual perception using software and hardware.</p>
<p>Computer Vision is important in all sorts of areas, like:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Automating processes</strong>: Companies use computer vision to =
boost operational efficiency.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Healthcare</strong>: It helps analyze diagnostic images, making =
it possible to detect issues faster and more accurately.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Security</strong>: Surveillance systems use it to detect suspici=
ous activity in real-time.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Agriculture</strong>: Computer vision helps monitor crops and id=
entify possible plant diseases.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Autonomous vehicles</strong>: Self-driving cars rely on computer=
 vision to understand what=E2=80=99s happening around them, ensuring safe n=
avigation.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionConcepts-HowisanimageanalyzedusingComputerVision?">=
How is an image analyzed using Computer Vision?</h2>
<p>Computer Vision applications get their input from sensor devices like ca=
meras, but it could also be a medical imaging device, a video camera or any=
thing else that captures images. They identify patterns in the data and use=
 those patterns to interpret the content of other images.</p>
<p>These applications work with algorithms that are trained on huge amounts=
 of visual data or images stored in the cloud to mimic how the human visual=
 system works. To do this, the image goes through a pattern recognition pro=
cess where it=E2=80=99s broken down and compared to a library of known patt=
erns. This helps determine if something in the image matches something prev=
iously identified by the system.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionConcepts-WhatisComputerVisionusedfor?">What is Comp=
uter Vision used for?</h2>
<p>Computer Vision can be used in all sorts of applications and sensor devi=
ces to serve different practical purposes, like:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Object classification</strong>: The application classifies the o=
bjects in an image based on their category. So for example, it can tell the=
 difference between people, cars, trash cans, and other objects in a photo.=
</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Object identification</strong>: When it comes to identifying obj=
ects, the application focuses on picking out a specific object in the pictu=
re. For example, the system not only distinguishes people in a photo, but a=
lso analyzes their features to determine whether someone is a child or not.=
</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Object tracking</strong>: The system analyzes a video to process=
 the location of an element (object) over time. This technology is often us=
ed in security systems to identify people and/or cars in a certain area and=
 provide info about their movements.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Text recognition (OCR =E2=80=93 Optical Character Recognition)</=
strong>: The system finds letters and numbers in images and converts them i=
nto a machine-encoded format, so that other applications can read and use t=
hat information.</p></li>
</ul>
    </div>
</body>
